{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"The AWS MCP Server now supports cross-account and cross-role access - AWS","context":"AWS announced the addition of cross-account and cross-role access support for the AWS Model Context Protocol (MCP) Server, which is part of the Agent Toolkit for AWS. This feature enables developers using AI coding agents like Kiro, Claude Code, or Codex to work across multiple AWS accounts and IAM roles within a single session without the need for restarts. Previously, switching profiles necessitated stopping the AI coding session, updating local AWS credentials, and restarting the MCP server for every account change. Now, AI agents using the AWS MCP Server can specify a profile on each command, allowing users to switch between accounts and roles seamlessly. Cross-account access facilitates faster development in multi-account environments, enabling tasks such as querying CloudWatch logs across production and staging accounts or updating a Lambda configuration in one account and adjusting an S3 bucket policy in another, all within the same conversation. The AWS MCP Server is currently available in the US East (N. Virginia) and Europe (Frankfurt) Regions. To get started, refer to the Multi-profile support section in the Agent Toolkit for AWS user guide","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/06/aws-mcp-server/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"ingesting-into-data-lake","skill_description":"Import data into the AWS data lake from S3 files, local uploads, JDBC databases (Oracle, SQL Server, PostgreSQL, MySQL, RDS, Aurora), Amazon Redshift, Snowflake, BigQuery, DynamoDB, or existing Glue catalog tables (migration). Default target is S3 Tables; standard Iceberg on a general purpose bucket is supported where S3 Tables is not adopted. Handles one-time loads, recurring pipelines, migrations. Triggers on: import data, load data, ingest, sync database, migrate table, move data to AWS, set up pipeline, ETL, pull from Snowflake, query BigQuery into S3, export DynamoDB, CTAS, convert to Iceberg. Do NOT use for setting up or troubleshooting Glue connections (use connecting-to-data-source), creating empty tables (use creating-data-lake-table), running queries (use querying-data-lake), finding tables by fuzzy name (use finding-data-lake-assets), catalog audit (use exploring-data-catalog), or SaaS platforms like Salesforce, ServiceNow, SAP, MongoDB, Kafka.\n\n\nServices: glue, s3, s3tables, athena, dynamodb\nTasks: deploy, migrate\nPersona: developer, data-engineer\nWorkload: data-analytics\n*","skill_name":"ingesting-into-data-lake"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"AWS Data Exports now supports cross-account delivery - AWS","context":"AWS Data Exports now supports cross-account delivery, allowing FinOps teams to send Standard exports, including Cost and Usage Report 2.0 (CUR 2.0), FOCUS, Cost Optimization Recommendations, and Carbon Emissions reports, directly to any authorized AWS account's Amazon S3 bucket. This eliminates the need for customers to replicate data across accounts or pay for duplicate storage. The destination account owner controls which source accounts can deliver data through S3 bucket policies. This feature is available in all commercial AWS Regions, except the AWS GovCloud (US) Regions and the China Regions. By using this capability, FinOps teams can configure CUR 2.0 exports from their management account to flow directly into a centralized analytics account, without building custom replication processes, and adhere to the security best practice of keeping non-administrative workloads out of management accounts","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/03/aws-data-exports-cross-account-delivery-cost/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"setting-up-cloudtrail-multi-region","skill_description":"Enables a multi-region AWS CloudTrail trail with S3 log storage, CloudWatch Logs integration, and CloudWatch Logs Insights queries for security monitoring and compliance auditing. Use when setting up centralized API activity logging across all AWS regions.\n\nServices: cloudtrail, s3, cloudwatch, iam, kms\nTasks: configure, secure, monitor\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: security, monitoring\n*","skill_name":"setting-up-cloudtrail-multi-region"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"connecting-lambda-to-dynamodb","skill_description":"Connects an AWS Lambda function to DynamoDB with IAM roles, stream event source mapping, and read/write permissions. Use when setting up Lambda-DynamoDB integration, processing DynamoDB stream events, or deploying serverless event-driven architectures.\n\nServices: lambda, dynamodb, iam, cloudwatch\nTasks: deploy, configure\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: serverless, database\n*","skill_name":"connecting-lambda-to-dynamodb"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify AWS docs on Forecast S3Config RoleArn same-account requirement", "parent_id": "f2d5e307-9407-4c85-b896-d2aeed3cb437"}
